 \subsection{Reanalysis and precipitation products}
 \label{sec:rw_data}
 
-Recent advances in meteorological modeling have been supported by the increasing availability of high-resolution reanalysis datasets and the development of machine learning techniques, both of which have become important for precipitation forecasting, as demonstrated by \citet{10512739}. Reanalysis datasets provide temporally and spatially consistent climate information that supports retrospective climate studies and weather prediction applications \citep{10512483}. Among these products, the ERA5 reanalysis dataset developed by ECMWF under the Copernicus Climate Change Service represents a substantial improvement over its predecessors. \citet{hersbach2020era5} introduced ERA5 with hourly coverage and a spatial resolution of approximately 31~km, showing marked improvements over ERA-Interim in reproducing variability and extremes across multiple atmospheric variables. Subsequent evaluations have further clarified the reliability and limitations of ERA5. \citet{mcnicholl2021evaluating} found strong agreement between ERA5 near-surface air temperature and ground-based observations but also reported discrepancies under complex meteorological conditions. \citet{doddy2021reanalysis} compared several reanalysis products for renewable energy applications in Ireland and showed that ERA5 generally outperforms MERRA-2 and JRA-55, while still exhibiting regional biases that require consideration. For precipitation, \citet{lavers2022evaluation} evaluated 24-hour totals against 5,637 stations over 2001--2020, finding a general wet bias, lower annual maximum one-day amounts, and smaller random errors in winter than summer in the extratropics. These daily-scale findings do not establish the accuracy of hourly precipitation over Dublin. \revminor{The Copernicus European Regional Reanalysis (CERRA) provides atmospheric reanalysis data over Europe at a horizontal resolution of 5.5~km, offering finer spatial detail than the global ERA5 reanalysis \citep{ridal2024cerra}. Although \citet{